+
+### 8.2 Proof-grade cross-document order
+
+A compiler import seal completes on an `Exported` verdict, so the verdict it reads (`NamespaceExportOracle::proven_export_at_original`) needs order that is a fact in every run, not navigation advice. `RunOrder::proven_precedes(event, query)` supplies it beside the navigation relation, which stays as §8 and §8.1 describe. Within one document it is the §8.1 relation with `modeled_source_exclusion`. Across documents it answers `Some(true)` only when `event`'s command has completed before `query`'s starts in every run, and never `Some(false)`: a loader may be evaluated again, so an event later in one pass can precede the query of the next.
+
+`WorkspaceIndex` builds the proof with `RunOrder::with_entry_proof` over the same edges as the navigation order. A document entered once more by code the index cannot see breaks the claim: tclsh runs `main.tcl` before `exports.tcl` through `uplevel #0 [list source main.tcl]`, an alias of `source`, `$child eval [list source main.tcl]`, `eval source main.tcl`, `after 0 [list source main.tcl]`, a renamed `source`, or a `package require` whose index sources it, and a namespace deleted through `$cmd delete ::src` is invisible to the deletion inventory. So nothing is proven across documents unless every indexed document has a source-order context and every command of every document, definition bodies and callbacks included, is visible (`ProofInventory`, classified from selected Registry facts only):
+
+- no command word is dynamic;
+- every command word without selected Registry words names a procedure or class some indexed document declares, and no command word names a tail a rename may have moved a command to;
+- no command may evaluate code the inventory does not retain: every script-carrying operand (`Body`, `Expr` with a substitution, `LambdaLiteral`, `OpaqueScript`) is a literal word containing a retained script region, a `ScriptBodyFlow::ConcatenatedScript` takes a single script word, no `CommandPrefix` operand or retained callback exists (`authored_source_retains_callback`), and no command creates an alias, binds or renames commands under dynamic operands, or changes the working directory (`PackageResolverDependency::WorkingDirectory`); words whose schema cannot be read count as all of these;
+- every command that may enter a file (`BodyExecutionSpec::SourceFile`, `Traits::LOADS_EXTERNAL_UNIT`, or a package requirement) is the original site of a resolved edge, so an unresolved `source $dir/x.tcl` or a `package require` with no resolved provider turns the proof off.
+
+A document then has a *proven entry* when exactly one edge reaches it, a `source` edge whose command is a §8.1 sequence position directly in its parent's root script, outside every definition body. The root-script rule keeps the child in the parent's frame and namespace: tclsh evaluates a file sourced inside `namespace eval foo` in `::foo`, so its `namespace eval src` creates `::foo::src`.
+
+`event` precedes `query` when all of the following hold:
+
+- both are load-level sequence commands of their documents (`SourceOrderContext::load_command`). A body, branch, loop, caught script or callback event is never proven;
+- following proven entries upward from both documents reaches a common document, with no cycle on either chain;
+- in that document, the event side's entering `source` (or the event itself, when it is written there) is a sequence command that ends before the query side's entering `source` (or the query) starts. Reaching the later command means the earlier one returned normally;
+- in each document below the common one on the event side, no possible early exit starts before the end of the point that matters there: the event, or the `source` entering the next document down. Every command that cuts the §8.1 sequence or withholds its end counts as such an exit, so a `return`, a user procedure that may `return -code return`, or another `source` before the export leaves it unproven. The entering `source` itself is allowed: if its file returns early, the event's own exits already account for that.
+
+The common document may itself be entered any way and any number of times, conditionally included: the claim holds within each of its evaluations. A document entered from two sites (a re-sourced file), from a procedure body, from a branch or `catch`, or also through `package require` has no proven entry and can only be a common document.
+
+tclsh 8.4.20–9.1.0 agree on every shape: a loader that sources `exports.tcl` and then `main.tcl` beside itself imports the exported command, whatever the working directory, and a conditional, caught or procedure-local `source`, an early `return` or a procedure doing `return -code return` before the export, a nested `source` of a file doing `return -code return`, a `namespace eval foo` around the `source`, an intervening `namespace export -clear` or `namespace delete`, re-sourcing `main.tcl` first and running `main.tcl` alone do not. `source` of a file doing `return -code return` returns code 2 to its caller, and of a file with a top-level `break` code 3; `catch {source x}` around an error raised after the export still leaves the export in place, but a caught source is never a sequence command.
+
+The verdict also needs the export row's `unconditional_load_level` marker, recorded from the document's own sequence when the row is indexed, and no namespace deletion in the window. `SourceOrderContext` keeps the start of every command whose selected Registry transitions contain `NamespaceTransition::Delete` or widen `StateTransitionDomain::Namespaces`; with no name resolution, any such command that is not proven before the export or after the import makes the verdict unknown.
+
+How the program starts cannot be decided from the source. The user decided it (`docs/work/name-handling/handoff/reviews/resume-20261017-diagnosis/user-decisions-20261019T0710Z.json`: entry scripts, working directory, indexed path, configuration and relative paths), and the proof is anchored on the host's configuration, which `WorkspaceIndex::set_source_order_configuration` installs and `RunOrder::with_entry_proof` reads as an explicit input (`SourceOrderConfiguration`: each workspace folder and its `[project] entryPoints` from `.tcl-lsp.ini`, the same list the W120 inheritance reads):
+
+- **Entry scripts.** tclsh can run a sourced document as a script of its own (`tclsh main.tcl` gives LOCAL), which never runs the loader's earlier files. In a folder that configures `entryPoints`, the listed files are its entry scripts and every other document is sourced only: a listed file is never a proven entry even when another file sources it, and order is proven only below a document a listed entry reaches through `source` or `package require`, so an unlisted loader proves nothing. When one of a folder's configured entries is not an indexed document (a typo, another spelling of the same path) or cannot be placed at all, it may be the real entry script, so nothing in that folder is proven. In a folder that configures none, a sourced document is assumed not to be run as a script except a document with a `#!` line or that names the interpreter's script-name variable (`NativeBootstrapVariable::Argv0`, the `[info script] eq $argv0` guard; before Tcl 9.0 an unqualified `argv0` in any namespace falls back to the global one, so its name there counts too), and such a document is never a proven entry.
+- **Working directory and path resolution.** C Tcl's `source` resolves a relative path against the process's current working directory, never against the sourcing file's directory and never through `auto_path`, `TCLLIBPATH` or `libraryPaths` (tclsh 8.4.20–9.1.0: a relative `source exports.tcl` in `lib/inner.tcl` loads the working directory's `exports.tcl`, and `source x.tcl` with `x.tcl` only on `TCLLIBPATH` or `auto_path` fails). The proof therefore reuses the edges of the host's configured source resolver and admits an edge only when its path names its file without the working directory: an absolute path, or one built from `[file dirname [info script]]` (`OriginalSourcePathExpression::rooted_without_working_directory`), resolved against the sourcing document's indexed path. A loader is invoked at its indexed path, so a symlinked invocation from another directory is outside the premise. A relative literal `source` proves nothing, configured entry scripts included: the working directory is wherever the script was launched from (tclsh 8.4.20–9.1.0: an entry doing `source exports.tcl`, run from its parent directory, sources the parent's `exports.tcl` and gives LOCAL).
+
+  Any command whose Registry transitions change the working directory (`cd`) or the script path (`info script filename`, `PackageResolverDependency::ScriptPath`, which re-points `[file dirname [info script]]` for the rest of the evaluation: tclsh then gives LOCAL) turns the proof off. Library configuration reaches the proof only through `package require`: a required package must have an indexed provider for the require to be a placed edge.
+
+Premises shared with every single-document proof: each document's root script runs at level `#0` in the global namespace when its own analysis is read; code the workspace does not index (an unindexed `pkgIndex.tcl`, a library procedure reached as a command, the auto-loader's `tclIndex` files reached through `unknown` or `auto_import`) neither re-enters a document nor deletes a namespace; and a command whose Registry transitions are undeclared deletes no namespace.
+
+Controls (unexecuted source definitions, WorkspaceIndex): `a_loader_proves_its_first_source_ran_before_the_second` (also through a nested `source` and under a conditionally sourced loader; reversed order abstains), `a_conditional_or_caught_source_proves_nothing`, `a_source_in_a_procedure_or_another_namespace_proves_nothing`, `a_re_sourced_file_proves_nothing`, `no_loader_or_an_unplaced_source_proves_nothing`, `an_export_after_a_possible_early_exit_proves_nothing`, `hidden_evaluation_anywhere_proves_nothing` (every shape above, `cd`, `info script filename`, and an `eval $script` in a document nobody sources), `a_marked_entry_script_proves_nothing`, `a_namespace_local_argv0_read_marks_a_script_where_it_falls_back_to_the_global`, `only_a_working_directory_independent_source_proves_order` (relative gives none; absolute and `[file dirname [info script]]` paths prove), `configured_entry_points_start_the_proof`, `a_loader_no_configured_entry_reaches_proves_nothing`, `a_configured_entry_is_never_a_proven_child`, `a_folder_with_an_unindexed_or_unplaced_entry_proves_nothing` and `a_package_the_library_configuration_provides_keeps_the_proof`, each also pinning that the navigation relation stays unmodeled and that no foreign pair is proven the other way round. Verdict controls: `a_loader_sourcing_the_export_first_proves_the_forced_import`, `both_tiers_shadow_when_another_file_holds_the_covering_export`, and the counterexamples `a_namespace_deleted_after_the_export_proves_nothing` and `a_conditional_or_body_export_proves_nothing_to_the_compiler`.
